# Separate Rust Tests

Production `src/` must not contain test code or test-only infrastructure.

## Rules

- **No test utilities in production code** — no `test-utils` features, memory/mock backends, capture sinks, or `#[cfg(test)]` hooks wired into production APIs.
- **Never inline** test bodies in production files: no `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { ... }` or `#[test]` inside production logic.
- **`lib.rs` is a thin crate root** — module declarations and `pub use` re-exports only. No test declarations, no business logic.
- Declare sibling test modules from the **production file they test**, not from `lib.rs`: `logger.rs` → `#[cfg(test)] mod test_logger;` + `test_logger.rs`.

## Sibling test files (default for unit tests)

For each production file `<name>.rs`, add a sibling **`test_<name>.rs`** in the same directory.

```text
src/packages/logger/src/
├── lib.rs              # mod backend; mod logger; pub use logger::*;
├── logger/
│   ├── mod.rs          # production code + #[cfg(test)] mod test_logger;
│   └── test_logger.rs
```

```rust
// logger/mod.rs (bottom of file)
#[cfg(test)]
mod test_logger;

// logger/test_logger.rs
use super::{log, LogLevel, Logger};
```

If a behavior cannot be exercised from a sibling test file **without** adding production test hooks, **skip that unit test** — do not add test-only code to production modules.

## Integration tests (`tests/`)

Use `tests/*.rs` at the crate root only when the scenario truly cannot live in a sibling file (e.g. cross-binary integration). Do not add production features or backends solely to support `tests/` integration tests.

| Case | Location |
| --- | --- |
| Unit tests (preferred) | `src/test_<source>.rs` sibling to the source file |
| Integration (rare) | `tests/*.rs` at crate root |
